# Normalize everything to LF in the repository.
# Research-integrity requirement: specs/ hashes and artifacts/ fixtures appear
# in provenance blocks (spec §4.4). CRLF renormalization would silently change
# those hashes and invalidate reproducibility claims.
* text=auto eol=lf

# text=auto, not unconditional text: unconditional `text` converts line endings
# "without guessing the content type" (gitattributes(5)), which would override
# line 5 and strip binary detection from exactly the trees this file protects.
# The binary list below covers only 5 extensions, so a .jpg/.ico/.pdf/.jar under
# corpus/** would be silently LF-mangled - and Task 4 verifies the pinned SHA
# against the cache clone before copying the subtree, so nothing re-checks the
# vendored bytes afterwards.
specs/**       text=auto eol=lf
artifacts/**   text=auto eol=lf
corpus/**      text=auto eol=lf

# Unconditional `text` is deliberate here and is NOT an inconsistency with the
# three rules above: .json is a known-text extension, so forcing LF is precise
# and intended. Do not "tidy" this to text=auto.
tools/*.json   text eol=lf

# PowerShell is the one exception - keep CRLF on Windows.
*.ps1 text eol=crlf

*.png  binary
*.zip  binary
*.gz   binary
*.exe  binary
*.tar  binary
